The natural attraction you're describing is **La Bufadora**, a spectacular marine geyser (blowhole) located on the **Punta Banda Peninsula** in **Baja California, Mexico**, about **27 km (17 miles) south of Ensenada**.

When Pacific waves crash into an underwater sea cave at the base of the cliffs, water and compressed air are forced upward through a natural fissure, creating powerful spouts that can reach **30–100 feet (9–30 meters) high** with a thunderous roar. Travel sources consistently describe it as the **second-largest marine geyser in the world** (after one in Oahu, Hawaii) and a major tourist draw on the peninsula.

